<section class="im-section im-wrap">
	<div class="im-prose">
		<h2>The buy box sticks</h2>
		<p>On a long product page the decision is made a long way from the top — somewhere around the third screenshot or the second question. A price that scrolled away ten screens ago is a price the buyer has to go and look for, and going to look for it is where the sale is lost.</p>
		<div class="im-table-scroll">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th>Class</th><th></th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td><code>im-rating</code></td><td>Stars as <strong>one</strong> element with a clipped overlay, not five glyphs — five spans is five things to keep in sync and a half star is impossible with them. <code>--im-rating</code> is the score; the accessible text is the number.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-ratingsum</code> · <code>im-ratingbar</code></td><td>The distribution. A 4.9 average with the 1-star row visible is more persuasive than a 4.9 on its own, because it shows nothing is hidden.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-review</code></td><td>One review. <code>im-review-verified</code> is "bought it" — the only claim worth making about a reviewer.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-buybox</code></td><td>The panel. <code>position: sticky</code> under the top bar, in the <a href="/collections/post/single/">single layout</a>'s aside.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-buybox-price</code></td><td>The price at display size, with the old one struck through beside it.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-licences</code> · <code>im-licence</code></td><td>One of several, each a row with its own price. <code>aria-checked</code> is the chosen one — the same fill-and-ring the rest of the system uses for "current".</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-buybox-list</code></td><td>What is in the box. Ticks in the success colour, drawn as masks so they are right in both themes.</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>im-buybox-note</code></td><td>The small print that stops a refund request. Put the refund terms <em>here</em>, not on a policy page.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		</div>

		<h2>Details that matter</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Two buttons, not one.</strong> The second is for the person who is not ready — "browse the docs" keeps them on the site instead of closing the tab.</li>
			<li><strong>Answer the objections on the page.</strong> The four questions in the accordion are the four emails you would otherwise get; a product page that makes somebody ask has already cost more than it saved.</li>
			<li><strong>Never invent the price in the template.</strong> It comes from whatever takes the payment. A page that says $149 while the checkout says $199 is a refund and a bad review.</li>
			<li><strong>The screenshots open.</strong> Somebody spending $149 wants to look closely; the carousel is wrapped in <code>data-im-lightbox</code>.</li>
		</ul>
	</div>
</section>
